<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duncan Stewart (colonial administrator)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Duncan Stewart was a British colonial administrator who became the second governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown Colony</a> of Sarawak in 1949. Within weeks of his arrival he was stabbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosli-dhobi">Rosli Dhobi</a>, a member of the movement opposing Sarawak's cession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, during a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibu">Sibu</a> in December 1949, and he died of his wounds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Duncan George Stewart was born on 22 October 1904 at Witkleifontein in the Transvaal Colony, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, to British parents posted there. He was educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at Winchester College and Oriel College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he took a BA. He was married and had three children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Stewart joined the Colonial Administration Service in 1928. He served as a district officer in the Oya Territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, as colonial secretary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, as secretary of finance in Mandatory Palestine and as secretary of the governors' conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. In 1948 he was made a Companion of the Order of St Michael and St George. His record was regarded as exceptional, and Lord Listowel, minister of state for colonial affairs, named him governor and commander-in-chief of Sarawak in succession to Charles Arden-Clarke.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Only a couple of weeks after taking office, Stewart made his first official visit within the colony, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibu">Sibu</a>, on 3 December 1949. Press reports described large and cheerful crowds. After he had inspected an honour guard and met a group of schoolchildren, a youth, Moshidi bin Sedek, approached holding a camera and asking for a picture, and as Stewart posed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosli-dhobi">Rosli Dhobi</a> stabbed him. Both youths were arrested on the spot.</p><p>Despite a deep wound, Stewart is reported to have tried to carry on until blood seeped through his white uniform. He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuching">Kuching</a> for treatment and then flown to the General Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, where he died on 10 December 1949. The two youths and two other conspirators were convicted of his murder and hanged.</p><p>Documents released in the late 20th century show that the British government knew that Anthony Brooke, whom opponents of the cession hoped to restore to the throne of Sarawak, had not been involved, but kept silent so as not to provoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, newly independent after its war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, while the UK was already facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duncan Stewart (colonial administrator)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The anti-cession movement culminated in the assassination of Governor <b>Duncan Stewart</b> at Sibu by Rosli Dhobi in December 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/crown-colony-of-sarawak">Crown Colony of Sarawak</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Transvaal Colony. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=1.3,103.8&amp;year=1933" aria-label="Singapore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7433 2115 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Duncan Stewart (colonial administrator)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7883 2385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7883 2385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=1.3,103.8&amp;year=1933">What was here in 1933? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
